From 2d36c957319cdcacd8556295f68e58bb75a362c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: proshiv85-byte <proshiv85@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 08:35:08 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 24/34] WIP: ASoC: samsung: add an Exynos8895 ABOX driver

Boot the vendor Calliope firmware on the ABOX Cortex-A7 and use its
IPC protocol (messages in ABOX SRAM, SGIs through the GIC inside ABOX)
to play PCM through RDMA0. The AP programs CMU_ABOX (PLL_AUD and the
dividers), the routing and the UAIF0 format, like the vendor driver
does. UAIF0 is the I2S clock master.

The firmware sees fixed IOVAs (DRAM at 0x80000000, IVA, VSS, the dump
area and the PCM buffer). The buffers come from dma_alloc_coherent on a
device behind the SysMMU, so they are already mapped at some IOVA; the
driver maps their pages again at the addresses the firmware expects.

Playback goes RDMA0 -> SPUS mixer -> SIFS0 -> UAIF0 at 48 kHz. The
vendor's ASRC in that path isn't set up for the stream rate by this
driver and drained the RDMA about four times too fast, so it is left
out. The firmware only signals the periods (RDMA0_BUF_EMPTY), not the
position: that comes from the RDMA status, which tells which of two
periods is read and how far, so the buffer is kept at two periods.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic)
